<p><strong>Warning on the 13.3 figure.</strong> The number 13.3 does appear in my data, but it is not an ESR: it is <code>worst_abs_pct</code>, the worst monthly budget variance (2026-05, forecast under actual by 13.3%) [qid:4f560380]. Setting a GCP ESR target against "13.3" without first confirming which of the two quantities the card means risks anchoring a commitment decision on a forecast-error percentage. I am flagging this rather than resolving it, because the AWS-side qid is outside my desk.</p>
<h2>2. ESR as it actually stands</h2>
<p>The measured position, unchanged: 2026-07 billed 2552.92 USD, effective 2323.03 USD, ESR 9.0% [qid:6681bdbe]. Commitment purchase sat at 700.00 USD in both 2026-07 and 2026-08 [qid:9ef9f242][qid:73ee955c], so nothing in the purchase line moved between the two months.</p>
<p>The stated boundary on this metric still holds and is the reason a lift cannot be sized: <strong>coverage and utilization against the 80% waterline require a commitment inventory that is not in the datasets</strong> [qid:6681bdbe]. Without it, I can report the effective-rate spread but cannot attribute it, cannot say which commitments are under-utilized, and cannot say what headroom a lift would fill.</p>
<h2>3. Realized versus modelled saving</h2>
<p><strong>Realized: 118.28 USD</strong>, the billed-minus-effective spread for 2026-08 (billed 1270.34, effective 1152.07), month-to-date through 2026-08-15 [qid:70accc39][qid:b8954e40]. This is a partial month and must not be presented beside a full month, nor annualized here.</p>
<p><strong>Modelled: none available.</strong> No component of the lift package carries a saving estimate in the supplied figures.</p>
<p>One correction worth making before this reaches a report: the month-over-month service deltas are <strong>not</strong> realized saving. Compute Engine shows -446.47, GKE -274.12, Cloud SQL -220.82 against July [qid:596c5ae7], but August is partial through the 15th [qid:b8954e40]. Those deltas are a half-month-versus-full-month artifact. Booking them as lift results would overstate the package by roughly the whole calendar effect.</p>
<h2>4. Idle scheduling (task #67 component)</h2>
<p>The weekend signal is real and measured: weekday average 68.79 USD/day against weekend average 55.45, a weekend ratio of 0.806 [qid:9cbac9e2]. Weekend load runs at about four fifths of weekday load, which means the dip is shallow: a large fixed floor is running through Saturday and Sunday.</p>
<p>Where the floor most likely sits, by billed weight: Compute Engine 473.11, GKE 266.92, Cloud SQL 201.12 [qid:ce571a15]. These three are the only services with a service-level figure in the optimizer block.</p>
<p><strong>What I cannot finish here.</strong> The card asks me to complete the remaining services from #67's 7-of-12 review. The dataset gives me at most six named services [qid:596c5ae7] and no record of which seven were reviewed or what the twelve are. The five outstanding services are not identifiable from what I hold, so the idle-scheduling component is incomplete, not merely unsized.</p>
